steps.body:
Give each feed handler thread its own message counter and the threads never touch each other's data, yet they can run several times slower than one thread alone. That's the **false sharing** from *Memory Layout: Alignment, Padding, and Cache Lines*: a core must own a whole cache line to write any byte of it, so two counters on one line bounce it between cores. That lesson laid out padded slots of plain `long`s. Here the counters are `std::atomic`s, bumped by real threads, so you can watch it happen.

```cpp
struct alignas(64) PaddedCounter {
    std::atomic<int64_t> n{0};
};

std::cout << sizeof(PaddedCounter) << '\n';  // 64
```

`alignas(64)` matches the 64-byte lines of x86-64 servers. As that lesson found, 128 is the safe pad when one binary must also suit Apple Silicon, whose lines are 128 bytes, and the example below shows why on this sandbox. Padding costs memory, so pad only what different threads write often. The cheapest fix is often the one from *Threads, Data Races, and Mutexes*: count in a local variable and add it to the shared total once.

example.code:
#include <atomic>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

struct Counter {
    std::atomic<int64_t> n{0};
};

struct alignas(64) PaddedCounter {  // one cache line each
    std::atomic<int64_t> n{0};
};

template <typename C>
int64_t count_messages(int k, int n) {
    std::vector<C> counters(k);  // thread i only ever touches counters[i]
    std::vector<std::thread> handlers;
    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {
        handlers.emplace_back([&counters, i, n] {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
                counters[i].n.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
            }
        });
    }
    for (std::thread& t : handlers) {
        t.join();
    }
    int64_t total = 0;
    for (const C& c : counters) {
        total += c.n.load();
    }
    return total;
}

int main() {
    int k = 0;
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> k >> n;
    std::cout << "sizeof " << sizeof(Counter) << " and " << sizeof(PaddedCounter) << '\n';
    std::cout << "alignof " << alignof(Counter) << " and " << alignof(PaddedCounter) << '\n';
    std::cout << "packed " << count_messages<Counter>(k, n) << '\n';
    std::cout << "padded " << count_messages<PaddedCounter>(k, n) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

